<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.1.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02329</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.1.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>deb03346-2f28-4f13-94b6-cbe93c619e64</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><emphasis><emphasis>
    <para>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</para>
  </emphasis></emphasis>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> expressions that are perfect squares.</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \((x+5)(x+5)\) </item>
  <item> \((-9+c)(c-9)\) </item>
  <item> \((y-10)(10-y)\) </item>
  <item> \((2x-1)(2x+1)\) </item>
  <item> \((4-3x)(3-4x)\) </item>
  <item> \((a+b)(b+a)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((x+5)(x+5)\), \((-9+c)(c-9)\), \((a+b)(b+a)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Each diagram represents the square of an expression or a perfect square. Complete the cells in the last table by
    answering questions a–d.</item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-between">
  <para><emphasis>Diagram 1</emphasis>
    <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2259d051936f1271547bf944c3af61764012fc1b"/></media></figure></para>
  <para><emphasis>Diagram 2</emphasis>
    <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/bc6b5acb2e2e95c69fe1de6cca7a0b4184fdccd9"/></media></figure></para>
  <para><emphasis>Diagram 3</emphasis>
    <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6a71f86eade1ae48e7c211562e3823e1f5e4c409"/></media></figure></para>
</div>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the answer for Cell a in Diagram 3?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(\frac {1}{3}h\) </item>
  <item> \(h^2\) </item>
  <item> \((\frac {1}{3}h)^2\) </item>
  <item> \((\frac {1}{3})^2\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(h^2\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the answer for Cell b in Diagram 3?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(\frac {1}{3}h\) </item>
  <item> \(h^2\) </item>
  <item> \((\frac {1}{3}h)^2\) </item>
  <item> \((\frac {1}{3})^2\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(\frac {1}{3}h\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the answer for Cell c in Diagram 3?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(\frac {1}{3}h\) </item>
  <item> \(h^2\) </item>
  <item> \((\frac {1}{3}h)^2\) </item>
  <item> \((\frac {1}{3})^2\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(\frac {1}{3}h\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the answer for Cell d in Diagram 3?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(\frac {1}{3}h\) </item>
  <item> \(h^2\) </item>
  <item> \((\frac {1}{3}h)^2\) </item>
  <item> \((\frac {1}{3})^2\) </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Rewrite the perfect-square expression \((n+7)^2\) in standard form \(ax^2+bx+c\).</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(16n+49\) </item>
  <item> \(n^2+14n+14\) </item>
  <item> \(n^2+49n+14\) </item>
  <item> \(n^2+14n+49\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(n^2+14n+49\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Rewrite the perfect-square expression \((5-m)^2\) in standard form \(ax^2+bx+c\).</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(-12m+10\) </item>
  <item> \(m^2+10m-25\) </item>
  <item> \( m^2-10m+25\) </item>
  <item> \(25m^2-10\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( m^2-10m+25\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Rewrite the perfect-square expression \((h+ \frac {1}{3})^2\) in standard form \(ax^2+bx+c\).</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(2h+\frac {2}{3}h+\frac {1}{9}\) </item>
  <item> \(h^2+\frac {2}{3}h+\frac {1}{9}\) </item>
  <item> \(h^2+\frac {2}{6}h+\frac {2}{6}\) </item>
  <item> \(h^2+\frac {1}{9}h+\frac {2}{3}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(h^2+\frac {2}{3}h+\frac {1}{9}\)</para>
<para> For questions 6 - 9, solve each equation, then choose the <emphasis>two </emphasis>solutions.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((x-1)^2=4\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(x = -1\) </item>
  <item> \(x = 5\) </item>
  <item> \(x = 2\) </item>
  <item> \(x = 3\) </item>
  <item> \(x = 6\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x = -1\), \(x = 3\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((x+5)^2=81\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \( x = 76\) </item>
  <item> \(x = -14\) </item>
  <item> \(x = 86\) </item>
  <item> \(x = 3\) </item>
  <item> \(x = 4\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x = -14\), \(x = 4\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((x+11)^2=121\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(x = 132\) </item>
  <item> \(x = 110\) </item>
  <item> \(x = 0\) </item>
  <item> \(x = -22\) </item>
  <item> \(x = 4\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x = 0\), \(x = -22\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((x-7)^2=\frac {64}{49}\)</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(x = 2\) </item>
  <item> \(x=- \frac {279}{79}\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \frac {41}{7}\) </item>
  <item> \(x= \frac {407}{49}\) </item>
  <item> \(x = \frac {57}{7}\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x= \frac {41}{7}\), \(x = \frac {57}{7}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>Solve the equation \((x-2)^2=0\).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=2\)</para></section>
